class:: CoupledMapLattice
summary:: Periodic lattice of diffusively coupled logistic maps
categories:: UGens>Generators>Chaotic>Maps
related:: Guides/Stochastica, Classes/CoupledLogisticMap

description::

A Coupled Map Lattice extends the concept of coupled chaos to a discrete spatial grid. It mathematically simulates a one-dimensional array of logistic maps, where each cell's next state is a weighted average of its own chaos and its immediate neighbors. Conceptually, this models phenomena like heat diffusion, chemical reactions, or moving waves of turbulence. In audio, it generates massive, spatialized modulation data, allowing you to map a propagating wave of chaos across multiple filters, spatial speakers, or granular parameters.

Implements a one-dimensional periodic coupled-map lattice. Each cell mixes its logistic image with the images of its left and right neighbors. Two preallocated arrays make each trigger update atomic.

The outputs are raw state coordinates. They are not normalized audio signals and may diverge for some parameters. The technique follows the coupled-map-lattice literature.

Like link::Classes/Stepper::, each cell holds between trigger edges; unlike Stepper, all 2–64 outputs are updated from a coupled periodic neighborhood.

subsection:: Space-time view

image::coupled-map-lattice-space-time.png#A 48-cell lattice over 400 iterations with r = 3.88 and coupling = 0.14. Cell position runs horizontally and time downward; the restrained light-blue-plum scale encodes state from low to high.::

classmethods::

method:: ar
argument:: numCells
Init-rate output count, clipped to 2 through 64.
argument:: trig
Advance the complete lattice on a rising edge.
argument:: r
Logistic coefficient sampled at an event.
argument:: coupling
Diffusive neighbor-coupling coefficient; not clipped.
argument:: initialBuf
Buffer with at least code::numCells:: samples. Missing data uses 0.5.
argument:: reset
Reread the initial Buffer on a rising edge.
returns:: code::numCells:: audio-rate outputs.

method:: kr
Control-rate version with the same arguments and output count.

instancemethods::

examples::

code::
(
Routine {
    b = Buffer.loadCollection(s, Array.fill(8, { 0.2.rand + 0.1 }));
    s.sync;
    x = { LeakDC.ar(Splay.ar(CoupledMapLattice.ar(8, Impulse.ar(200), initialBuf: b) * 0.03) )}.play;
}.play;
)
::

code::
(
Routine {
    var cells = 16;
    b = Buffer.loadCollection(s, Array.fill(cells, { 0.2.rand + 0.3 }));
    s.sync;
    x = {
        var lattice = CoupledMapLattice.ar(cells, Impulse.ar(120), 3.88, 0.14, b);
        Splay.ar(SinOsc.ar(lattice.linexp(0.05, 0.95, 80, 6000), 0, 0.008))
    }.play;
}.play;
)
::

code::
// Spatially Diffusing Turbulence
(
{
    var trig, lattice, freqs, sig;
    trig = Impulse.kr(12);
    // A 1D grid of chaotic cells diffusing into their neighbors
    lattice = CoupledMapLattice.kr(8, trig, coupling: 0.2);
    freqs = lattice.linexp(0, 1, 100, 3000);
    // Map the wave of chaos to an array of resonant filters
    sig = Resonz.ar(WhiteNoise.ar(0.5), freqs, 0.1);
    Splay.ar(sig) * EnvGen.kr(Env.perc(0.01, 0.1), trig)
}.play;
)
::
